            { title: "Uncertainty is the problem", body: "The syllabus opens with a claim about the world: **uncertainty** is what makes decision making difficult. It gives three running examples.\n\n- **Healthcare:** for a set of patients, we do not know which patient will experience an acute event.\n- **Finance:** given a set of assets, we do not know which ones will experience a significant increase in price.\n- **Retail:** we do not know how much demand there will be for a given product next week.\n\nWithout uncertainty, the syllabus argues, good decisions would be trivial: we would know exactly which patient to prioritize, which asset to invest in, and how much of a product to stock. Everything else in this course exists because we do not know." },
            { title: "Prediction is the response", body: "The course's response to uncertainty is **prediction**. For each example, the syllabus names the prediction that would help:\n\n- **Healthcare:** predict each patient's **probability** of experiencing the acute event.\n- **Finance:** predict **how much** each asset's price will change.\n- **Retail:** predict **how much demand** there will be for the product.\n\nTwo shapes of target are already visible in the syllabus: a probability of an event, and a quantity. And predictions are made **with the help of data**: patient characteristics in healthcare, previous prices in finance, social media interest in retail." },
            { title: "Machine learning, as this course defines it", body: "The syllabus defines the field in one sentence: **machine learning is the science of using data to create models for making effective predictions.**\n\nThat definition frames all five lectures. Each session introduces a class of models, and every one of them is a way of turning data into predictions. When a later topic feels abstract, come back to this sentence and ask what data goes in and what prediction comes out.\n\nThere is **no required textbook**. Slides and materials come through BruinLearn; the syllabus lists recommended books (The Analytics Edge; An Introduction to Statistical Learning; Hands-On Machine Learning with Scikit-Learn and PyTorch; Deep Learning with Python) as optional support." },
            { title: "Judging quality and complexity", body: "Building a model is only half the course. The syllabus says the five lectures also develop **analysis of their quality and complexity**: how good a model's predictions are, and how complicated the model is that makes them.\n\nSo hold two questions for every model class you meet: how well does it predict, and at what complexity? The syllabus does not define the measures yet. They arrive with the lectures, starting Nov 4." },
            { title: "Five lectures, one family: supervised learning", body: "The syllabus groups the whole course under **supervised learning**. Its overview names the model classes in order:\n\n- **Linear regression** and **logistic regression** (Class 1, Wed Nov 4)\n- **Regularized regression**, then an introduction to **tree models** (Class 2, Fri Nov 13)\n- **Tree models**, including the **tree ensemble** models the overview names (Class 3, Wed Nov 18)\n- **Neural networks** (Class 4, Wed Nov 25, and Class 5, Wed Dec 2)\n\nThe schedule warns that the topic assigned to each week is approximate and may change as the quarter progresses. Slides for each class will be posted on BruinLearn; none are held yet, which is why this topic is orientation only." }
